        "cumulative_resource_use": "One fresh training run requested at 120000 steps, completing 120832 rollout-aligned steps, and one 160-episode research evaluation. No official assessment requested; no best-known or retained model role assigned.",
        "current_goal_gap": "M1 achieved 61.25% (98/160), leaving a large gap to 98%. The dominant measured failure is failure to reach: 62 episodes failed, 58 without any tolerance entry and only 4 after entering tolerance. Performance is strongly radius-dependent: 40/40 successes at 6-10 cm, 50/53 at 10-14 cm, 8/39 at 14-18 cm, and 0/28 at 18-20 cm. The policy therefore does not yet solve the outer-workspace portion of the official distribution, even though its hold behavior is usually successful once it reaches the target.",
        "current_synthesis": "Observed evidence: expanding training from the prior 14-20 cm range to the full 6-20 cm range produced T1 training success of 0.60 and M1 research success of 61.25%. M1 shows a monotonic outer-radius collapse: every sampled target below 10 cm succeeded, while no target from 18-20 cm succeeded. Among failures, 58/62 never entered tolerance and 4/62 entered but did not complete the hold; thus the present gap is primarily an approach/reach gap, not evidence of a general hold instability. Interpretation: the model's prediction that near-extended configurations and Jacobian conditioning matter is supported, but the data do not identify whether the cause is outer-workspace singularity and braking, a learned inverse-kinematic branch/basin failure from the extended reset, or insufficient optimization coverage of the narrow outer boundary. Angle-quartile variation is smaller than the radius effect, so angle is not the leading discriminator. A useful policy result does not establish its cause, and M1 does not yet establish that any particular branch or torque mechanism is responsible.",
        "decision_frontier": "For outer targets near 14-20 cm, is non-reach caused primarily by geometry-conditioned control failure near the extended Jacobian singularity (including branch choice, saturation, and stopping dynamics), or by insufficient learning of the outer-target mapping? Discriminating evidence must expose episode trajectories or equivalent joint/action/Cartesian telemetry, stratified by radius, so that failed episodes can be separated into wrong-branch motion, saturation/overshoot, and stalled or insufficiently directed motion. A candidate trained with an intervention should only be credited as progress if it improves this outer-radius failure mode without sacrificing the already strong inner-radius hold behavior.",
        "evidence_references": [
